  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1245 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nLong-term forecast impacts: Warm temperatures and low rain chances \non Thursday and Friday, and a frontal passage expected over the \nweekend.\n\nDiscussion: Broad southwest flow pattern continues through the end \nof the week. The forecast trend over the last 24-48 hours is for \nsubtropical ridging to be a little stronger over Mexico/far south \nTexas than previously expected, which seems to be guiding the \nmidlatitude jetstream track a little further north. Because of that, \nany embedded waves in the flow on Thursday/Friday look to be more\nglancing, and associated PoPS with them are low. Highs near 70 \nand lows in the 50s are anticipated.\n\nIt still looks like a major frontal passage will occur sometime over \nthe weekend (likely Sunday). With that said, the jet's northward \ntrend increases the likelihood that the frontal passage will either \nbe dry, or that rain chances will be confined to storms immediately \nalong or behind the front, rather than a synoptic-scale rain event. \nPoPS and exceedance probabilities have accordingly dropped. That \nfront will drop temperatures back toward (perhaps a little above) \nclimatological normals for the early part of next week.\n\nMeister",
